visualizing cultures is a web-based  history program which John dower and I  started in year 2001 John dower is an  eminent historian of modern Japan he won  a Pulitzer Prize in 1999 for his book  embracing defeat I approached John in in  2000 when I knew that OpenCourseWare was  about to go up and I told John that I  thought that this would be a great  platform to to do something new with  history specifically to look at history  not just from text which is what  historians do but from looking at images  visuals that have been produced by by  people from the historical era and to  try to understand what was going on John  surprisingly said yes and so we started  we didn't quite know what you know we  were doing at first but we got very  lucky with our first unit which is  Commodore Matthew Perry's arrival in  Japan in 1853 to open up Japan which had  been essentially isolated closed for 250  years and so you have America in Japan  come classing together in this one  moment in history and from that  explosion of images from both sides you  know Americans depicting the Japanese  and Japanese depicting Imperium his men  and his ships it's just wonderful what's  really interesting is that when you look  at images you get to see things that you  cannot get from written material we  found lots of images where the Japanese  were really curious about various men  and drawing  costumes instruments weapons in my new  detail and with with notes to go along  with them showing just sort of sheer  curiosity who are these people you know  how do they know how do they talk you  saw the same thing on parasite Perry  brought his own artist Heine who was a  26 year old artist trained in Germany in  sort of romantic style and he just fell  in love with Japan yeah you can tell  that in the images he absolutely fell in  love with this beautiful country so much  so that he would sometimes paint himself  into the picture  yeah interacting with the Japanese and  so what you got was this one artist  depicting Japan in a very beautiful very  affectionate way and that's what the  Americans saw of Japan for the first  time this is why even to this day you  know in this country you have this image  of Japan as very beautiful very fun very  beautiful nature and really originates  with highness paintings and so we they  must follow the OCW principle of not  only making it available and open and  free but that everything is licensed  under Creative Commons so that these  images which we got from about 200  museums and be some major museums Boston  MFA Sackler in Washington Hong Kong  British Museum we asked all of them to  sign an agreement that we could present  their images on open on now visualized  in Japan on OpenCourseWare under  Creative Commons and so anyone coming in  and studying or visualizing cultures can  take images and copy them distribute  them alter them into their own material  and use them you know however the  wish one of the populations that we  really wanted to work with or school  teachers and so we did many many  workshops over the years and we trained  face to face around 2,000 teachers this  is we I said we we worked with a  wonderful group out of the universe of  Colorado that specializes in creating  East Asian curriculum and working with  teachers directly to help them to  incorporate them into their classroom  and so they ran this series of workshops  all over the country and as far as I  know it's still being used so high  school teachers university professors  tell us that they're using it on a  regular basis  a couple years ago a university had an  opening for assistant professor in  Japanese history and they had four  finalists and they asked them how do you  teach Japanese history and three the  three of them apparently said they used  visualizing cultures
 

